## Core CMP patterns

A strong CMP usually centers on a self-service catalog, approval workflows, orchestration, governance, observability, and cost management rather than just a dashboard. Industry guidance specifically highlights service catalogs, approval-based provisioning, cloud brokerage, and unified policy control as core platform capabilities. [omg](https://www.omg.org/cloud/deliverables/CSCC-Practical-Guide-to-Cloud-Management-Platforms.pdf)

For your use case, the closest equivalent to YouTube’s “creator ecosystem” is a **blueprint publishing** model: platform engineers define reusable offerings once, and consumers request them through a catalog without touching provider-native complexity directly. That abstraction layer is a standard capability in multicloud platforms and fits your Terraform-oriented control-plane model well. [perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/search/6a72ba9f-cea8-4b6b-9284-fb8f36972f7f)

## Security-oriented translation

The YouTube split between public pages and blocked application paths maps cleanly to a CMP design where public docs stay open but all operational surfaces require authenticated, authorized access. In your context, that should pair with scoped provider identities, secret-store-based credential resolution, and fine-grained authorization instead of global credentials or broad admin roles. [perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/search/b9091615-8da9-4a2a-946b-b5b3db0c4623)

A practical CMP equivalent of “policy & safety” is a cross-cloud governance engine that enforces tagging, approvals, access constraints, compliance checks, and remediation hooks before and after provisioning. That aligns with common multicloud platform expectations and with your existing direction around OpenFGA, Vault, and brokered multi-cloud access. [perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/search/4394d9e4-d6ef-4f30-a34b-7b0fc57757a6)

## Suggested domain model

A useful decomposition for your portal would be:
- Service Catalog: approved offerings, versions, parameters, and pricing metadata. [docs.matrix42](https://docs.matrix42.com/approving-and-provisioning-services/3504955_approval-process)
- Provisioning Orchestrator: Terraform or provider-adapter execution, state transitions, retries, and post-provision hooks. [cloudbolt](https://www.cloudbolt.io/multi-cloud-management-platforms/)
- Governance Layer: approval flows, policy-as-code, tagging, quota, and compliance checks. [ituonline](https://www.ituonline.com/blogs/comparing-multi-cloud-management-platforms-features-benefits-and-selection-criteria/)
- Resource & FinOps Inventory: discovered assets, drift, cost allocation, forecasts, and showback/chargeback. [corestack](https://www.corestack.io/blog/multi-cloud-management-platform/)
- Identity & Secrets: tenant-scoped authz/authn, provider account delegation, and secret retrieval at runtime. [perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/search/32c7e10e-3094-4e2d-9d3f-2b0a58bd0642)

